From u5140016d70f6ba82 at groups.google.com  Fri Sep 25 18:54:14 2009
From: u5140016d70f6ba82 at groups.google.com (Jeff Laake)
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2009 18:54:14 +0000
Subject: Re: Bootstrap variance with g(0)
Message-ID: <7a6WraeW4pw.MmsL3Z3bn0UJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <7a6WraeW4pw.F3-knRGODZIJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
References: <7a6WraeW4pw.F3-knRGODZIJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/distance-sampling/c/7a6WraeW4pw/m/MmsL3Z3bn0UJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Yes.  There is no way to include generically a non-parametric bootstrap
of g(0).  It could be included parametrically but there really is no
logic or reason to do so.  Instead, you can take the std deviation of
your  bootrstrap abundances and add in the variance for g(0).

var(D*g(0))= (D*g(0))^2*[cv^2(D) +cv^2(g(0))]

where cv^2(D) = (bootstrap std dev of D)^2/D^2

--jeff

